  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"Volunteers have stated their availability and task preferences; a coordinator can cover small gaps, and brief handovers fit within every shift.\", \"decision\": \"How should volunteers receive their working assignments?\", \"goal\": \"Help usable household goods reach interested neighbors while keeping contributions, claims, and final collection understandable.\", \"problem\": \"Neighbors are organizing a one-day fair for clean, usable household goods that people no longer need. Giving and taking are both optional, and no payment or matching contribution is required. Volunteers have intake space, category tables, reserve shelves, a holding area, carts, and simple item records. Contributors choose in advance whether unclaimed goods should be returned during the closing collection period or donated through an arranged pickup.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nThe coordinator assigns tasks when volunteers arrive, using their stated preferences and the current workload at each station. Duties respond to conditions on the day while remaining within the time and types of work volunteers have offered.\nThe coordinator assigns volunteer shifts from the availability and preference list and circulates the resulting roster before the fair. People can prepare for specific duties, and the coordinator can see uncovered work before the event begins.\nVolunteers claim open shifts on a task roster, and the coordinator fills any remaining gaps from unassigned availability. People choose suitable duties directly while the completed roster still gives the fair a clear coverage plan."
    }
  ],
